  "summary": "An extremely elaborate and ornamental style of Baroque decoration applied to architecture.",
  "description": "Churrigueresque features intricate, sculpt-like surface ornamentation with twisted columns, solomonic columns, and elaborate floral motifs, creating a sense of movement and theatricality. It developed as Spanish architects created increasingly complex decorative facades to express religious devotion and colonial power.",
  "culturalContext": "Churrigueresque — Spanish and Mexican Ultra-Baroque named for the Churriguera family of Salamanca sculptors — piled estípite pilasters, inverted obelisks, saints and vines over retablos and church facades from the 1680s to mid-1700s. Mexico City's Sagrario Metropolitano, Oaxaca's La Soledad and Taxco's Santa Prisca show its colonial apex; the style signaled Counter-Reformation triumph and silver-rich patronage.",
